    "# variables"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "58e08e4d-60f8-4f12-8ff8-73c8015d593b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "variables are fundamental building blocks that allow us to store, manipulate, and reuse data in our code"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0bfe5e46-a82c-490b-86a2-fdd7603fbadc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Rules for Python Variable Names\n",
    "When naming variables in Python, follow these rules:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Use only alphabets, numbers, and underscores (A-Z, a-z, 0-9, and _). Example: user_age, total_count2\n",
    "2. Start with a letter or underscore, not a number. Valid: _count, name1 Invalid: 1name\n",
    "3. No spaces allowed. Valid: first_name Invalid: first name\n",
    "4. Case-sensitive: age, Age, and AGE are three different variables.\n",
    "5. Avoid using Python keywords (like if, for, while) as variable names."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "5a7c476e-bb18-4130-a4eb-c212849b4984",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "25\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "x=25\n",
    "print(x)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "32a4fe18-ab1e-4348-92e3-3c4bcd01f51b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
